    {
      "id": "f28d24ba-d38b-4333-8ba7-a91698a7ae7f",
      "phaseId": "66b0de41-be11-474f-badf-f2b9b934eaf9",
      "number": 424,
      "shortId": "CTXQH",
      "priority": 3,
      "shortName": "agents-md-how-to-structure-and",
      "title": "AGENTS.md § How To Structure and Build a Component — the exposé, written up as the pattern",
      "status": "done",
      "description": "**Antonio, 2026-08-13:** *\"I want you to write a section in AGENTS.md: How To Structure and Build a Component. and describe here what to do (the way you did with Exposé). create a folder under chrome: chrome/the_component and inside it split in smaller reusable component like react. Describe the best practices and pattern to do that.\"*\n\n# What to write\n\nA new numbered section in `AGENTS.md`, near § 0b (widget vs component) and cross-referenced from it — § 0b says *where* a component lives and *that* it must be one; this says **how to build one**, as a worked recipe someone can follow without having read this session.\n\nThe subject is the exposé (F003/P082/T420), which is the first surface built this way and the reference implementation:\n\n```\nheca/src/chrome/<surface>/\n├── mod.rs            host wiring — the ONLY file that may touch AppState\n├── model.rs          the session reduced to plain data\n├── <leaf>_card.rs    the smallest piece\n├── <group>_card.rs   … composed of the leaf\n├── <row>.rs          … composed of the group\n└── testing.rs        #[cfg(test)] shared fixtures\n```\n\n## The points it must make, each with the exposé as the example\n\n1. **Split by what each piece OWNS, not by size.** `PaneCard` owns what a card *is*; `ColumnCard` owns the vertical share; `WorkspaceRow` owns the horizontal share and the float rects; `ExposeGrid` owns the row share and the cursor. Every question has exactly one owner, which is what makes a wrong answer findable.\n2. **Properties are struct fields; the constructor is a struct literal.** `PaneCard { pane_id, name, active, ws_idx, col_idx, next, theme, cb }.build()` — Flutter/SwiftUI named parameters, not an eleven-argument function (`providers/workspaces/pane_card()` is the counter-example, at eleven positional arguments).\n3. **The seams travel as ONE group, not as N arguments per level** — `ExposeCallbacks { choose, delete, cursor_to, dismiss, keys }`, built once by `callbacks(emit, keys)` so a test can supply exactly the app's edges and nothing else.\n4. **Never `&AppState`** — it needs a window, so a component that takes it cannot be tested. Only `mod.rs` sees it, and its job is to *gather* and hand down.\n5. **Return the concrete widget, not `Box<dyn Component>`, when the parent still has to size it.** A box has no builders left; `PaneCard::build -> (Row, GridCell)` is what lets a column give it a share and a row give it a rect.\n6. **A component that produces navigable cells returns them beside its widget** — `(Flex, Vec<GridCell>)` — rather than reaching into a registry.\n7. **Sizes are shares** (`Length::Pct` of one shared denominator, `grow` weights); the parent sizes the child, never the child itself. Include the gap-vs-padding trap: a gap is added *outside* a percentage width, so air between percentage-sized siblings belongs in their padding, while a gap between `grow` siblings is exact.\n8. **One headless test per component**, plus the box test for anything sized by shares: lay it out and assert it never exceeds what it was given.\n9. **`testing.rs` holds the fixtures** so each test reads as the thing it asserts.\n10. **It moves to `heca/src/components/` on the SECOND caller** — a move, never a copy.\n\n## Also\n\n- Point § 0b at it, and point `heca/src/components/mod.rs`'s module doc at it, so the three do not drift.\n- Keep it a recipe, not a rationale essay — the file is already long and this is the part an agent has to be able to follow at speed.\n\n# Verification\n\nAntonio reads it. The test of the section is whether a fresh agent could build a second surface from it without asking.\n\n---\n**Completion summary:**\n**Written 2026-08-13** — `AGENTS.md` § **0b-bis. HOW TO STRUCTURE AND BUILD A COMPONENT — the recipe, with a worked example**, sitting between § 0b (widget vs component) and § 0c (the event system). Uncommitted on `feat/palette-modes`.\n\nIt opens with the folder — *\"a surface is a folder, not a function\"* — and then eight numbered steps, each with the exposé worked through it:\n\n1. **Split by what each piece OWNS, never by size** — with the exposé's four components as a table of what each owns *and what it owns nothing about*, against the 400-line `map()` that owned every term at once and was wrong seven times.\n2. **Properties are struct fields; the constructor is a struct literal** — Flutter/SwiftUI named parameters in Rust's spelling, against `providers/workspaces/pane_card()`'s eleven positional arguments.\n3. **The seams travel as ONE group** — `ExposeCallbacks` + `callbacks(emit, keys)`, and why: that function is what a test calls to get the app's edges and nothing else.\n4. **Never `&AppState` outside `mod.rs`** — the single rule that decides whether the surface can be checked without photographing the app.\n5. **Return the concrete widget when the parent still has to size it** — a box has no builders left, so boxing forces the child to size itself, which is how a component starts computing geometry. Plus: navigable cells come back beside the widget, never through a registry.\n6. **Sizes are shares; the parent sizes the child** — with both traps written out: `grow` alone always fills, and a gap is added *outside* a percentage (so air between percentage-sized siblings belongs in their padding), plus the `flex: 1 1 0` idiom written once per surface.\n7. **One headless test per component, plus the box test** — `the_whole_map_never_exceeds_the_box_it_is_given` named as the one that would have caught all seven failures; fixtures in `testing.rs`.\n8. **It moves to `heca/src/components/` on the second caller.**\n\nCross-referenced both ways so the three cannot drift: § 0b now ends with a pointer to it, and `heca/src/components/mod.rs`'s module doc names it as the full recipe and marks its own list as the short form.",
      "descriptionUpdatedAt": "2026-08-13T07:58:07.085Z",
      "notes": "",
