    {
      "id": "2b99d64d-2c7d-4d11-831f-c94a72aea714",
      "phaseId": "66b0de41-be11-474f-badf-f2b9b934eaf9",
      "number": 467,
      "shortId": "MVH3E",
      "priority": 4,
      "shortName": "folderline-s-gap-and-icon-size",
      "title": "FolderLine's gap and icon size are raw pixels — make them tokens before they are copied",
      "status": "planned",
      "description": "**Small, and it matters because the file is shared.** `heca/src/components/folder_line.rs` (new 2026-08-21) carries:\n\n```rust\nconst GAP: f32 = 6.0;\nconst ICON: f32 = 12.0;\n```\n\nThey were moved **verbatim** from `heca/src/providers/workspaces/pane_row.rs`, where the cwd line was written inline, because the extraction had to be a move and not a behaviour change (the sidebar's row and the exposé's card now share the component). But AGENTS.md is explicit: sizes and colours come from tokens — \"hardcode nothing … border width, radius, glow\" — and this now sits in `components/`, which is the shelf other surfaces copy from. Two callers today, and every future one inherits the literals.\n\n**Build.**\n- `GAP` → a `Spacing` token (`Spacing::Xs` is the gap the exposé's cards use); the gap between a glyph and its label is the same idea everywhere.\n- `ICON` → derived from the surrounding text rather than fixed: the line already takes `font_scale`, so the glyph should follow it (a metadata line at 0.85 of the body should not carry a body-sized glyph). Check what `Icon::size` expects and whether a theme font size is reachable from the component's `&GuiTheme`.\n- The colours are already tokens (`theme.colors.foreground`) — leave them.\n\n**Verification.** This changes how two surfaces look, so it is Antonio's eye, not a test: the sidebar's cwd row and the exposé card's folder line, at more than one font size (`prefix+Shift+r` after changing `[font] size`). A test can only assert that no literal remains.\n\n**Why here.** F011/P087 is where a shape becomes shared; this is the first component that arrived on the shelf carrying numbers from the surface it came from, and the rule is worth enforcing on the way in.",
      "descriptionUpdatedAt": "2026-08-21T11:28:37.851Z",
      "notes": "",
